<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amasis II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 526 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ahmose II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amasis II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 570 to 526 BCE, of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty. A general who took the throne after an army revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a>, he ruled prosperously, allied with Greek states and gave Greek merchants the trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> conquered Egypt months after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amasis-ii.jpg" alt="Amasis II" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_of_Amasis_II,_c._550_BCE._From_Egypt._Neues_Museum,_Berlin,_Germany.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Most information about Amasis comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and can be verified only imperfectly from monuments. According to the Greek historian he was of common origins, an officer in the Egyptian army born at Siuph near Saïs. He took part in a campaign of Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> in 592 BCE. His mother was a woman named Tashereniset, as a bust now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> shows, and a stone block from Mehallet el-Kubra names his maternal grandmother as Tjenmutetj.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Native Egyptian soldiers returning from a disastrous expedition to Cyrene in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> suspected that they had been betrayed so that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a> could rule more absolutely through his Greek mercenaries. Amasis was sent to quell the revolt but was proclaimed king by the rebels instead. Apries fled and was killed in a final attempt to regain power around 567 BCE, in the fourth year of Amasis, and an inscription records his honourable burial. Amasis married Khedebneithirbinet II, one of the daughters of Apries, to legitimise his kingship. In the same year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the evidence suggests they gained a foothold but were repelled.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes Amasis as a philhellene. He assigned the commercial colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> on the Canopic branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, contributed 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> to rebuild the temple at Delphi, and married Ladice, a Greek princess of Cyrene. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates of Samos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a>, added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to his kingdom, and adorned the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> with monolithic shrines. Herodotus writes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> then reached its highest prosperity, with 20,000 inhabited cities. When Cyrus destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in 546 BCE and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in 538 BCE, Amasis was left without major allies and cultivated closer ties with the Greek states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amasis died in 526 BCE and was buried at the royal necropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, within the enclosure of the temple of Neith. His tomb has not been rediscovered. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> reigned about six months and was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in 525 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a>. Herodotus relates that Cambyses had Amasis' mummy taken from its tomb, whipped and stabbed, and finally burned. In Herodotus' account the war began when Amasis sent Nitetis, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a>, in place of his own daughter to Cambyses, who then learned of the trick.</p><p>Stories of Amasis circulated from the fifth century BCE in Egyptian and Greek sources, presenting him as an unconventional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of practical wisdom, a trickster on the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> disrespected him as a commoner, he melted down a golden footbath into a statue that the people then worshipped. He then told them they now owed him the same respect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amasis II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Croesus built a network of alliances with <b>Amasis of Egypt</b>, Nabonidus of Babylon and Sparta, which he hoped would protect him against the rising power of Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His court attracted the poets Anacreon and Ibycus and the physician Democedes of Croton, and he made alliances first with <b>Amasis of Egypt</b> and later with Cambyses II of Persia, and around 522 BCE the Persian satrap Oroetes lured him to the mainland and had him killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
